- **src/context/ContentstackContext.tsx** (`ContentstackProvider`): This is where all CMS content is computed and provided to the entire React tree (236+ consumers via useContent). Currently it only passes through the static `defaultContent` prop with no mechanism to receive or apply live preview updates from Contentstack. Live preview requires the provider to subscribe to Contentstack's real-time content changes and re-render consumers — without that hook here, no downstream component can ever show preview content regardless of its own code.
  - **Minimal fix:** Integrate Contentstack's Live Preview SDK (`@contentstack/live-preview-utils`) inside ContentstackProvider: (1) add a `livePreviewData` state initialized to `{}`, (2) call `livePreviewInit` on mount to subscribe to preview events, (3) on each preview callback, merge the incoming preview entry into `livePreviewData` state, (4) change the context value to deep-merge `defaultContent` with `livePreviewData` so that preview fields override published fields. The `useMemo` dependency array must include the live preview state so consumers re-render on preview updates. This is the minimal change — no new components, no re-architecture, just a state + subscription added to the existing provider.


## Verification Criteria (what a tester will CONFIRM — your change must satisfy every one)
These are observable checks, derived from the acceptance criteria and description. They describe WHAT is observed, not how to build it. Make the minimal change that makes all of these true; your accompanying test should assert them:
- When a tester opens the application with Contentstack live preview enabled, the rendered page displays the CMS draft content, including edits that have not been published.
- After a tester edits a content entry in Contentstack while live preview is active, the change becomes visible on the displayed page without republishing the entry, rebuilding, or redeploying the application.
- When the application is opened without live preview enabled, the rendered page displays the published CMS content and no unpublished draft edits appear anywhere on the page.
- With live preview active, content rendered inside reusable components such as cards and links shows the draft edits the tester made to those entries, so no component on the page shows only the published version while preview is on.
- When live preview is active but the CMS connection or content update fails, the page continues to display its most recently shown content and does not render blank, crash, or show an error screen.

